Here's the formal test smell audit of your integration test suite using the **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog**.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` means one assertion path *never* executes per run. If the type is always `User`, the `PremiumUser` branch is dead code giving false coverage confidence.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_ReturnsStandardUser()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}
```

Write a *separate* test for the `PremiumUser` path with appropriate setup.

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden gap — if the branching condition changes, you silently lose test coverage on one path.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Problem:** No assertion — this test passes even if inserts silently corrupt data, insert duplicates, or insert zero rows. The framework considers "no exception" as success.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var all = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, all.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage metrics look good while nothing is actually verified.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Calibration note:** This class is `[TestCategory("Integration")]`, so external timing dependencies are *more tolerable* — downgraded from High to Medium. However, `Thread.Sleep(3000)` is still non-deterministic: too short on slow CI, wastefully long locally.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of fixed sleep
    var notified = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(notified, "Notification was not sent within timeout");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures and a 3-second tax on every run. Research (Camara et al., SAST 2021) correlates Sleepy Tests with flakiness.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Problem:** Four assertions with no message parameters. When one fails in CI, the error reads `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` — no context about *which property* was wrong without reading source.

**Fix:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist unchanged");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist unchanged");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis of CI failures; developers must look up source to understand what broke.

---

## ✅ Smell-Free Patterns (Well-Written Tests)

These tests are clean and well-structured:

| Method | Why it's good |
|--------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange/act/assert, focused scope, meaningful assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, single assertion, readable |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count contextually obvious (3 inserts → 3 count), uses `CollectionAssert` |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Tests a real integration concern (thread safety), asserts outcome |

The fixture design is also solid — in-memory SQLite avoids Mystery Guest (no external resource dependency), and `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` ensure proper isolation. Both fields are used by all tests, so no General Fixture smell.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | 2 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split into two focused tests | 5 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling/wait helper | 15 min |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Add message strings to assertions | 3 min |

**Bottom line:** 4 out of 8 tests are clean. The two High-severity findings (assertion-free and conditional logic) are the most urgent — they represent tests that can silently pass when behavior is broken. The overall structure (in-memory DB, proper setup/teardown, focused test names) is strong.
